To add email alert functionality, I will implement Nodemailer in the project. First, I will install the package:

```
npm install nodemailer
```

Then, I will create a function in `src/utils/alert.js` for sending email alerts:

```javascript
const nodemailer = require('nodemailer');

const sendAlert = async (recipient, subject, message) => {
  const transporter = nodemailer.createTransport({
    service: 'gmail',
    auth: {
      user: 'myemail@gmail.com',
      pass: 'mypassword'
    }
  });

  const mailOptions = {
    from: 'myemail@gmail.com',
    to: recipient,
    subject: subject,
    text: message
  };

  return transporter.sendMail(mailOptions);
};

module.exports = sendAlert;
```

Next, I will integrate this function in `src/monitor.js` where the downtime is detected:

```javascript
const sendAlert = require('./utils/alert');

const checkUptime = () => {
  // Logic to check uptime...
  if (downtimeDetected) {
    sendAlert('user@example.com', 'Downtime Alert', 'The server is currently down!')
      .then(() => console.log('Alert sent.'))
      .catch(error => console.error('Error sending alert:', error));
  }
};
```

Finally, I will update `config/settings.json` to include email settings. Here’s an example structure:

```json
{
  "email": {
    "service": "gmail",
    "user": "myemail@gmail.com",
    "pass": "mypassword"
  }
}
```

Once this code is implemented, I will test it by simulating downtime.
